  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1077",
      "html": "<ul><li>Scientific name: Ameiurus platycephalus (Girard, 1859)</li><li>Common name: Flat Bullhead</li><li>Size: 290 mm (11.4\") SL</li><li>Type locality: Savannah River drainage, near Anderson, South Carolina, U.S.A.</li><li>IUCN Red List: Least Concern (last assessed 2011)</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1077",
      "html": "<p>North America: Atlantic Piedmont and Coastal Plain from the Roanoke River drainage in Virginia, USA to the Altamaha River drainage in Georgia, USA.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1077",
      "html": "<p>Occurs in mud-, sand-, or rock-bottomed pools of small to large rivers. Also found in lakes, impoundments and ponds.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1077",
      "html": "<ul><li>pH: 6.6 - 7.8</li><li>Temperature: 18.0-26.0°C (64.4-78.8°F)</li></ul><p>Generally tolerant of water with low dissolved oxygen levels.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "equipment",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1077",
      "html": "<p>Open sand and rocks. Needs larger logs to hide under during the day.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1077",
      "html": "<p>A nocturnal feeder that feeds on mollusks, leeches, crayfish and tiny fishes.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1077",
      "html": "<p>Social with virtually anything they cannot swallow.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1077",
      "html": "<p>Females are much fuller bodied during the breeding season, which occurs in early spring.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1077",
      "html": "<p>Not reported in the aquarium hobby.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1077",
      "html": "<p>Identification: flat head, relatively straight snout profile. Relatively short anal fin, rounded in outline, 21-24 rays. Large dark blotch at dorsal fin base. Gold yellow to dark brown above, dark mottling on side, dull cream below, dusky fins, narrow black edge on median fins; 11-13 rakers on 1st gill arch.</p><p>Etymology: Ameiurus means \"curtailed\", referring to the lack of a deep notch in the caudal fin. Platys means flat and cephalus, head.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "references",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1077",
      "html": "<p>Proceedings of the Academy of Natural Sciences of Philadelphia v. 11 (nos. 60-77), pp 161.</p>"
    }
  ],
